static unsigned int list[32] __attribute__((aligned(64)));

__attribute__((noinline, noipa))
static int
contains (const unsigned int *p, unsigned int count, unsigned int start,
          unsigned int key)
{
  for (unsigned int i = start; i < count; ++i)
    if (p[i] == key)
      return 1;
  return 0;
}

int
main (void)
{
  return contains (list, 2, 1, 1);
}

when compiled on RISC-V with

-O3 -march=rv64gcv_zvl256b -mabi=lp64d -mrvv-vector-bits=zvl -mrvv-max-lmul=m2 -g0

shows

  bnd.9_47 = niters.5_27 - prolog_loop_niters.7_31;
  _63 = (unsigned long) bnd.9_47;

...

and so if niters is lower than prolog iters we get an underflow.
i.e.

  niters        = count - start = 2 - 1 = 1
  prolog_niters = 7

so when it calculates the remainder

  remaining = niters - prolog_niters;

this underflows:

   1 - 7 = 4294967290

Normally we don't get here for AArch64 and x86_64 because of the different
paths they take in vectorization.

For SVE we perform the loop fully masked, including trailing elements.
for Adv. SIMD and x86_64 we insert a peeling check.

  _46 = niters - 1 = 0;
  _48 = MAX(peel + 3, 5);   // at least 5

  if (0 < _48)
      goto bb26;

which skips the alignment peeling prologue.  Prior to
8af2e8e49d6e5d33c01c2beaead4933bc286974c we would have generated an epilog
for the early break and alignment peeling.

The change removed the requirement for an epilog for early break loops which
don't have side-effects, but still required alignment peeling.

This exposed that the checks in vect_do_peeling generate the epilog and so
the iteration check only when

  poly_uint64 bound_epilog = 0;
  if (!LOOP_VINFO_USING_PARTIAL_VECTORS_P (loop_vinfo)
      && LOOP_VINFO_PEELING_FOR_NITER (loop_vinfo))
    bound_epilog += vf - 1;
  if (LOOP_VINFO_PEELING_FOR_GAPS (loop_vinfo))
    bound_epilog += 1;

so if LOOP_VINFO_USING_PARTUAL_VECTORS_P then no epilog or check is generated.
This means that vect_gen_prolog_loop_niters needs to in itself handle this
assumption.

This is easy to do by generating a MIN (niters, prolog_niters) check.
If the guard is generated this will be optimized out, but if not,
it makes the prolog peeling loop handle the "scalar" iterations itself without
needing a second scalar loop.

